    "q-361712": {
      "dom_id": "step-q361712",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14138624/assets/a71e9121e38cfa60bfac0864ee2686cad756e268b78b512f55367bf6af0f8c94.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{3}{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{5}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]-[\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
